exports[`tmpl_close_awareness > with a lead 1`] = `
{
  "system": "You are closing a chapter of an ongoing story: you keep the story's records true to what the chapter established, one task at a time. This is an automated system. No one reads your replies or answers your questions, so act only through tool calls.

- Ids. Rows are named by short ids such as c1 or lo2, and the story's turns by ids such as t3. Use the ids you are shown, exactly as written; never invent one.
- Evidence. Every write gives "turns", the ids of the turns that show the change, and "why", one sentence on what in the cited turns makes the write true. Write only what the turns support; never invent.
- Turn ids. Cite turns only in "turns": never write a turn's id (t3) into any other text you send, "why" included. A turn id in a row's text would name a different turn in a later prompt, so a row's text holding one is refused.
- Reading. Write only to a row you have read in this task: one whose fields the task's start shows you, one read_row returned, or one you created in this task. Creating a row names a new one, so it needs no read.
- Results. Every write answers ok, rejected or skipped:
  - ok: it landed. A value already stored is ok too, with nothing written. A create's answer gives the new row's id. When some of its fields didn't land, the answer names each with its reason.
  - rejected: it didn't land, and the reason or its note says what to do. Fix the call and send it again, or leave that change.
  - skipped: the field stands as it is, because the user edited it after the turns you cited or a later turn wrote it. Don't write it again.
- Several calls. A reply may hold several tool calls; they run in the order you send them, and each gets its own answer.
- Finishing. When the task is done, reply without a tool call. A reply without a tool call ends the task, so until it is done, every reply calls a tool.

Wherever turns appear, the user's are marked with <user_turn> tags. Each is what the lead does, says or attempts; outside quoted speech, its "I" is the lead, as "you" is in narration told in the second person. Where the narration after a turn shows how an attempt turned out, the narration decides what happened, and what the lead says stands unless the narration contradicts it. A turn that plainly speaks to the narrator rather than playing the lead ("skip to the next morning") is a direction: it states nothing that happened, so take the facts from the narration.",
  "user": "# Your task: awareness

Each row below is what one character knows of a happening, with its pin: decay_resistance, from 0 to 1. As chapters pass a happening fades from what a character recalls, and the pin slows that; at 1 it never fades. With set_pin, change a pin only where this chapter shows it clearly wrong: most rows need no call.

- This chapter's rows: what a character learned during this chapter. Raise a pin toward 1 when the happening turned out to carry the chapter, something that character's story now turns on. Lower it toward 0 when it was pinned high but proved incidental.
- Rows that keep coming up: from any chapter, recalled often while this chapter was played. Raise a pin when the happening still carries that character's story; otherwise leave it. Never lower one of these: a lower value is refused.
- Raise a pin by at most 0.2 above the pin shown, in one close, never straight to 1: a bigger raise is refused, and a row that stays load-bearing climbs again at a later close.
- A row already pinned where it belongs needs no call: one near 1 that keeps coming up is already doing its job.
- Turns. No turn is shown here. Read the ones you need with read_turns: a row's "at" turn, or this chapter's turns, t1 to t5. Cite only this chapter's turns in "turns". A row's "at" turn outside that range is from an earlier chapter: read it with read_turns, never cite it.

The story's lead is Kael (c1). Outside quoted speech, "I" and "you" in the turns are the lead.

# This chapter
Kael wades toward the Drowned Keep and loses the sigil to the current; Mora watches from the stair.

# Rows

## hp1: The sigil lost in the flood
- aw1: Kael (c1), pin 0.4, learned this chapter, at t3, how: "it happened to him"
- aw2: Mora (c2), pin 0 (unset), keeps coming up

# Writes so far
What this close has written, in earlier tasks:
- merge_happenings The sigil lost in the flood (hp1): kept hp1",
}
`;

